meta {
  name: Unknown code
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/notacode
  body: none
  auth: none
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
  followRedirects: false
}

tests {
  test("answers 404 (FR-017)", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(404);
  });

  test("explains what the service is rather than showing an error string", function () {
    expect(res.body).to.contain("Snip turns long web addresses");
  });

  test("sets no cookie and forbids caching", function () {
    expect(res.getHeader("set-cookie")).to.be.undefined;
    expect(res.getHeader("cache-control")).to.equal("no-store");
  });
}

docs {
  FR-017 asks for a page that explains the service, not a bare error: for an
  anonymous visitor this is the most common first contact with the product
  (design.md 6.9).

  It is server-rendered HTML from an API-only Rails app, with the design tokens
  inlined and no webfont — the page has to render before anything else loads.

  A deleted link answers this same page rather than redirecting (FR-028): the
  row and the code survive so the statistics survive and the code is never
  handed to anyone else (FR-029, D12).

  ## The absence is cached too

  From Phase 3C a code confirmed absent is stored as the literal string
  `__404__` under `link:<code>` for 60 seconds (research.md D7). Walking the code
  space is ordinary traffic for a shortener, and without this every attempt is a
  Postgres query — `load/enumerate.sh` drives 60 000 requests at 500 absent codes
  and Postgres sees roughly 500 transactions, one per code rather than one per
  request (SC-007).

  Two consequences worth knowing when running this by hand:

  - Sending this request twice in a minute exercises the sentinel the second
    time. The response is identical, deliberately.
  - A link created within that minute on a code somebody had already probed
    still resolves immediately: creating a `Link` deletes the key in an
    `after_commit` callback (T058, D2), so the absence does not outlive the
    thing that made it wrong.
}
